# Trivy vulnerability ignore list
#
# Format: one CVE / advisory ID per line. Trivy auto-loads this file when
# present at the scan working directory. The aquasecurity/trivy-action
# honours it for both filesystem and image scans.
#
# Each entry must include a justification comment so future reviewers can
# decide whether the ignore is still warranted.

# node-ip — public IP detection package, transitive dep in our pnpm tree.
# Upstream has no patch available as of 2026-05; tracked at
# https://github.com/indutny/node-ip/issues. Risk surface is limited:
# we don't use it for SSRF protection or trust-boundary decisions.
# Re-evaluate when node-ip publishes a fix.
CVE-2024-29415

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pnpm install-time CVEs (pnpm 10.33.4)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Trivy flags pnpm via the `packageManager` / `engines.pnpm` field in
# package.json (copied into the built images). Every one of these advisories is
# an INSTALL-TIME issue in pnpm itself — malicious patch files, lockfile/alias
# path traversal, manifest-spoof lifecycle execution, configDependencies. The
# running API / web / portal containers never execute `pnpm install` (entrypoint
# is `node`), so pnpm is inert build-tooling in the image, not a live exploit
# path. Risk in the deployed artifact is effectively nil despite the HIGH rating.
#
# Proper remediation is a pnpm 10 -> 11.8.0 major upgrade (CVE-2026-55697 has no
# 10.x fix; the rest need 10.34.4). That is a repo-wide migration (lockfile +
# all Dockerfiles + workflow pins) requiring its own tested PR — tracked
# separately. Revisit / remove this block once that upgrade lands.
#
# Added 2026-06-27; re-review after the pnpm 11 upgrade.
CVE-2026-50015
CVE-2026-50016
CVE-2026-55487
CVE-2026-55697
CVE-2026-55698
GHSA-72r4-9c5j-mj57
GHSA-fr4h-3cph-29xv
GHSA-qrv3-253h-g69c

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# image-size 1.2.1 — CVE-2025-71329, CVE-2025-71330 (both HIGH, DoS)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pulled in by metro@0.84.4 (the React Native bundler) and nothing else —
# confirmed sole dependent in pnpm-lock.yaml.
#
# No fixed version exists upstream: Trivy reports both as `affected` with an
# empty "Fixed Version" column, so neither a pnpm override nor an upgrade can
# close them today. That is why these are ignored rather than bumped — unlike
# nanoid CVE-2026-67213 in the same Trivy DB refresh, which had a 3.3.17 fix and
# was closed with an override instead.
#
# Not exploitable in our artifacts: metro is a BUILD-TIME bundler for
# apps/mobile. It runs on developer machines and in CI, never in a shipped
# image and never on a server or customer device. Both CVEs are denial-of-
# service via a crafted image (a malformed ICNS for -71330) parsed by
# image-size. Reaching the vulnerable path means feeding a hostile image to our
# own bundler during our own build — i.e. the attacker already controls the
# source tree, at which point a bundler DoS is not the interesting problem.
#
# Remove when: image-size ships a fix, metro drops the dependency, or
# image-size appears outside the metro build chain. Re-check with:
#   grep -B30 'image-size@1.2.1' pnpm-lock.yaml
#
# Added 2026-08-07 (Trivy DB refresh during v0.104.0 release QA).
CVE-2025-71329
CVE-2025-71330
